{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792369981,"nonce":"A9xBfLZWjFV6q3kqGxTBGsbRSCY=","message":"Measured where the time goes between the last byte and the program starting (dashboard, 50 Mbit/40 ms): rewriting the program 540 ms (23 MB), compiling and instantiating it 20 ms, and loading the libraries 780 ms, nearly all of it blinc_abi's two rewrites (about 300 ms for the main-instance module and 370 ms for the thread module). Compiling is not the cost; the Rust-side rewrite is. Doing that work on the worker thread as the bytes arrive did not help: the thread that reads the response bodies stalled the downloads behind it, and the program arrived 0.5 s later. Preparing on an idle agent does: the program and each library are rewritten and compiled on an agent as they arrive. Page ready: 5.7 s sequential, 5.4 s parallel fetch, 4.6 to 4.9 s with agents (50 Mbit/40 ms); 12.5 s, 11.6 s, 10.8 s at 10 Mbit/80 ms. What is left after the last byte is the rewrite of whichever module arrives last, 0.5 s for the program; it would take a faster instrument pass (about 45 MB/s now) or splitting it across agents.","files":null}]}